{"lecture_id":"2026-07-07-M2-1","lecture_summary":"The lecture develops torque and lever-arm balance as the physical basis for center of mass, then applies weighted-position reasoning to point masses, grouped blocks, composite solids, and a rod with variable linear density. Key strategies include choosing a consistent origin, grouping components, checking the expected location, and converting density into a mass element before integrating.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Torque, Basic Introduction, Lever Arm, Moment of Force, Simple Machines & Mechanical Advantage","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jg4e8W44_E4","reason":"Introduces torque, lever arms, sign conventions, and seesaw balance, matching the lecture’s torque definition and equal-opposing-torque motivation."},{"rank":2,"title":"Center of Mass Physics Problems - Basic Introduction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2uszSnvzBEU","reason":"Covers weighted center-of-mass calculations for discrete masses in one and two dimensions, closely matching the two-mass and grouped-object methods."}],"coverage_gaps":["No strong playlist match explicitly covers continuous center of mass or the variable-density workflow dm = λ(x)dx, M = ∫λ(x)dx, including normalization of λ(x) = Cx.","No strong match explicitly demonstrates composite extended bodies by replacing each component with a point mass at its own center and finding component masses from m = ρV."]}
